//? Title: PCA on Iris: Axes, Metric and Reconstruction
//? Subtitle: Exact projection geometry, discarded energy and frozen preprocessing
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Several correlated measurements can describe nearly the same variation. We want a
smaller set of coordinates, with a measurable account of what their projection loses.
PCA chooses orthogonal directions that retain the most squared variation in a specified
feature metric. Four points make its axes and reconstruction error exact; a unit-change
example separates standardized error from original-unit error, and a wide two-row
example separates returned axes from numerical rank. Iris then illustrates score plots,
loadings, scaling choices and the eigenvalue-tail identity. A final split projects new
flowers through training-only means, scales and axes. Species colours are descriptive,
with no classification accuracy or biological importance inferred from variance shares.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Choose the origin and the feature metric

Let X have n rows and p numeric columns. Fit preprocessing once:

$$
 Z_{ij}=\frac{X_{ij}-\mu_j}{s_j},\qquad C=\frac{Z^TZ}{n-1}.
$$
\alttext(Each feature subtracts its fitted origin and divides by its fitted scale. The preprocessed cross-product matrix is divided by n minus one.)

With centring enabled, mu is the training mean. Without scaling, s=1 and C is the
sample covariance matrix. With scaling, s is the sample standard deviation, using
n−1, and C is the correlation matrix for nonconstant columns. Exactly constant centred
columns retain scale one and zero energy. They contribute no variance; they do not
magically become unit-variance features. The library defaults to centring and scaling.
R prcomp defaults to centring without scaling and rejects constant scaled columns.

If centring is disabled, mu=0 and C is a second-moment matrix rather than covariance.
Scaling then uses sqrt(sum(x_j squared)/(n−1)), not a centred standard deviation.
We use centred fits throughout the numerical examples. Changing a column's units
changes raw covariance PCA; standardizing each nonconstant column cancels positive
unit rescalings. Neither convention is universally more meaningful: scaling selects
a weighted squared-error objective, and small-variance noise can be amplified too.

The retained local TikZ source illustrates a perpendicular rank-one projection of the
four-point example below. Regeneration needs LaTeX with PGF/TikZ, standalone and Latin
Modern, plus dvisvgm. The exported report uses the saved image file.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/pca_iris_projection_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/pca_iris_projection.tikz > build/pca_iris_projection.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/pca_iris_projection_concept.svg build/pca_iris_projection.dvi > build/pca_iris_projection.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local PCA projection diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local PCA projection diagram.
// \image(figures/pca_iris_projection_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(PCA projects orthogonally in the chosen preprocessed coordinates, then reverses preprocessing for reconstruction.)
\alttext(Four points surround a zero mean. The first principal axis is the diagonal; point three comma one projects to two comma two with a perpendicular residual. A box states the score, projection and inverse-preprocessing steps.)

>> Maximize variance along a unit axis

A unit vector v has projected sample variance v-transpose C v. A Lagrange multiplier
for v-transpose v=1 yields Cv=lambda v. Maximizing that Rayleigh quotient chooses the
largest eigenvalue; repeating in the orthogonal complement selects the next axes.
For orthonormal columns V and descending eigenvalues lambda,

$$
 CV=V\Lambda,\qquad V^TV=I,\qquad T=ZV,\qquad
 \frac{T^TT}{n-1}=\Lambda.
$$
\alttext(The axes diagonalize the preprocessed covariance, scores multiply observations by those axes, and score cross-products recover the eigenvalues.)

The rotation columns are often called loadings, but they are not automatically
variable-component correlations. For standardized nonconstant variables those
correlations are V_jc*sqrt(lambda_c), when lambda_c is positive. Scores are not whitened:
their variances remain lambda. Eigenvector signs are arbitrary; a repeated eigenvalue
allows any orthonormal basis of its eigenspace. Compare projectors or reconstructed
geometry when checking implementations. Cutting through a tied eigenspace can give
different equally optimal projections, without identifying a preferred direction.

The native covariance eigensolver returns a full p-axis basis, including zero-energy
columns. It does not return only min(n−1,p) nontrivial components. Centred data have
rank at most min(n−1,p); `numerical_rank()` uses a scale-relative Gram-eigenvalue
threshold, 64*max(n,p)*machine_epsilon*lambda_1. All-zero energy has rank zero and
zero variance shares. Numerical rank is distinct from a scientific choice of k.

>> Derive projection loss from discarded variance

For the first k axes, the fitted affine reconstruction is

$$
 \widehat Z_k=ZV_kV_k^T,\qquad
 \widehat X_{k,ij}=\mu_j+s_j\widehat Z_{k,ij},\qquad
 \|Z-\widehat Z_k\|_F^2=(n-1)\sum_{c>k}\lambda_c.
$$
\alttext(Projection onto the leading axes reconstructs preprocessed rows. Reversing the scales and offsets returns original units. Total training squared error equals n minus one times the discarded eigenvalue sum.)

Write Z in its orthonormal axis basis. Each omitted score column contributes its
squared norm, (n−1)*lambda_c, with no cross terms. Retaining the largest such norms
minimizes rank-k squared error: the Eckart–Young result. Divide by n*p and take a
square root for RMS per entry, rather than dividing by n−1 again. `spectrum()` reports
the training tail; `reconstruction_curve(data)` measures residuals against the actual
input coordinates. All p axes reconstruct within floating-point error, not literal
exact-zero arithmetic. k=0 returns the fitted origin.

This optimality concerns preprocessed error. In original units it weights squared
coordinate residuals by s_j squared; a scaled fit need not be the best raw-unit rank-k
fit. A new query row can even have increasing original-unit error when one more axis
is retained, while its preprocessed error decreases. The tail identity uses the training
sample; query errors need not equal that same training eigenvalue tail.

>> Preprocessing and numerical limits are part of the fitted model

Fitting drops rows with a null selected feature and records their original indices.
Retained predictors must be finite. Transform/reconstruction require every query row
to be complete and finite, and use only the frozen training schema, means, scales and
axes. An individual row projects identically alone or as part of a batch. Missing
selected columns or mismatched types are rejected; extra columns are ignored.

Covariance is formed with extended-precision accumulation and normalized before the
Jacobi eigensolve, avoiding the eigensolver norm's overflow/underflow for representable
very large/small covariance scales. Forming a Gram matrix still squares the condition
number; near-null directions do not have SVD-level relative accuracy. Small negative
computed eigenvalues are clipped to zero. For n observations and p features the main
work is O(np^2+p^3) with bounded Jacobi sweeps and storage O(np+p^2); projecting m
new rows or sweeping their reconstruction prefixes costs O(mp^2).

> Simple examples

>> Four points give an exact eighty/twenty split

Use (-3,-1), (-1,-3), (1,3), (3,1), with no scaling. Their mean is zero,
covariance diagonal 20/3 and off-diagonal four. The axes lie along (1,1) and (1,-1),
with eigenvalues 32/3 and 8/3. Keeping the diagonal axis maps the negative pair to
(-2,-2) and the positive pair to (2,2). The four residuals have squared norm two each:
SSE=8 and RMS=sqrt(8/8)=1. Zero axes gives SSE=40; both axes reconstruct to roundoff.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pca_iris_four_points.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::stats;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{-3, -1, 1, 3});
  d.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{-1, -3, 3, 1});
  const PCA  fit(d, {"x", "y"}, PCAOptions{true, false});
  const auto r     = fit.reconstruct(d, 1);
  const auto curve = fit.reconstruction_curve(d);
  if (std::abs(curve.double_at("preprocessed_sse", 1) - 8) > 1e-12)
    throw std::runtime_error("projection reference mismatch");
  auto geometry = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  geometry
      .points(
          d.numeric_column("x").values(),
          d.numeric_column("y").values(),
          "observations",
          {37, 99, 235},
          5)
      .points(r.col(0), r.col(1), "rank-one projection", {220, 38, 38}, 6)
      .line({-3.5, 3.5}, {-3.5, 3.5}, "leading axis", {22, 163, 74})
      .title("Project onto the leading diagonal")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("y")
      .x_limits(-6.5, 6.5)
      .y_limits(-4, 6);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < d.nrows(); ++i)
    geometry.line(
        {d.double_at("x", i), r(i, 0)}, {d.double_at("y", i), r(i, 1)}, "", {107, 114, 128});
  auto error = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  error
      .line(
          curve.numeric_column("components").values(),
          curve.numeric_column("preprocessed_sse").values(),
          "measured SSE",
          {37, 99, 235})
      .points({0, 1, 2}, {40, 8, 0}, "exact tail", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .title("Discarding an axis discards its energy")
      .x_label("retained components")
      .y_label("squared reconstruction error")
      .y_limits(0, 55);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(geometry)
      .add(error)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/pca_iris_four_points.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n\nFull-basis residual SSE: {:.9e}.\n",
      fit.spectrum().to_md(9),
      curve.to_md(9),
      curve.double_at("preprocessed_sse", 2));
}
```
