\caption(PCA optimizes one declared metric; standardized and original-unit error need not move together on a new row.)
\alttext(Bars compare PC1 shares before and after changing one feature unit, with and without standardization. Relative query-error curves show preprocessed RMS decreasing while original-unit RMS initially rises.)

> Checking rank and normalization

>> Three returned axes can contain only one varying direction

Two rows (0,0,5) and (1,2,5) give three feature axes but centred rank at most
one. Sample standardization makes the first two columns equal and the third zero,
so the spectrum is (2,0,0). The constant column has offset five and divisor one.
A full basis is useful for an invertible coordinate description, but its null axes
have no training variance to explain. For entirely constant centred data every
variance share is zero; there is no meaningful one-hundred-percent leading axis.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pca_iris_rank.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::stats;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 1});
  d.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{0, 2});
  d.add_column("constant", std::vector<double>{5, 5});
  const PCA fit(d, {"x", "y", "constant"});
  if (fit.num_components() != 3 || fit.numerical_rank() != 1)
    throw std::runtime_error("rank reference mismatch");
  auto p = fit.plot_scree();
  p.title("Three axes, one varying direction").y_limits(0, 130);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(900, 430).save_svg("build/pca_iris_rank.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame prep;
  prep.add_column("feature", fit.feature_names());
  prep.add_column("offset", fit.means());
  prep.add_column("divisor", fit.scales());
  std::print(
      "Returned axes: {}; numerical Gram rank: {}.\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",
      fit.num_components(),
      fit.numerical_rank(),
      fit.spectrum().to_md(9),
      prep.to_md(9));
}
```
