\caption(Frozen training preprocessing makes a projection independent of the other rows in its query batch.)
\alttext(Training scores and thirty test projections share one two-axis plot. Training and test reconstruction RMS curves share the fitted training metric. Tables contrast fitted and unused test means and report all prefix errors.)

> Conclusion

The first two standardized Iris components retain 95.8132% of training energy,
with RMS reconstruction error .203933 in standardized coordinates and .188513 cm
in original units. The raw covariance fit retains 97.7685% of its own energy and
has .159189 cm RMS error at k=2. Those percentages concern different metrics.
On the fixed split, k=2 RMS in the training metric is .202648 for training rows and
.226351 for test rows; this one comparison does not select a generally optimal k.

The four-point calculation connects axes, eigenvalues and perpendicular projection
error. Unit changes and the new-row counterexample show why variance shares and
reconstruction loss need their metric. Full axis count is distinct from rank, and
null or repeated directions limit interpretations of individual loadings. Iris then
checks the same energy identity in four dimensions and applies frozen preprocessing
to separate rows. These are geometric representation diagnostics, not proof of a
biological signal, an optimal classifier or a universal retained-variance threshold.

> References

- Pearson, K. (1901). [On lines and planes of closest fit to systems of points in space|https://doi.org/10.1080/14786440109462720]. Philosophical Magazine 2, 559–572.
- Eckart, C. and Young, G. (1936). [The approximation of one matrix by another of lower rank|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02288367]. Psychometrika 1, 211–218.
- R Core Team. [prcomp|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prcomp.html]. Sample normalization, rotation/score conventions, sign ambiguity and the SVD implementation; the native implementation here uses a covariance eigensolver and explicitly differs on defaults and constant columns.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. Annals of Eugenics 7(2), 179–188. Iris measurement provenance.
